    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Formed from Upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#peru">Peru</a> in 1825 and named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sucre">Sucre</a> as constitutional capital and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#la-paz">La Paz</a> as seat of government.</li><li>Lost wars cost it the Pacific coast to Chile, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#acre">Acre</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#brazil">Brazil</a> and most of the Chaco to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#paraguay">Paraguay</a>.</li><li>Its 1952 revolution and the election of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#evo-morales">Evo Morales</a> made it a landmark of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#latin">Latin</a> American social and indigenous politics.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Landlocked since the War of the Pacific, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bolivia">Bolivia</a> still maintains a navy, which patrols <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lake-titicaca">Lake Titicaca</a> and the country’s rivers.</p></aside>
    </section>

