    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#antonio-jose-de-sucre"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antonio-jose-de-sucre.jpg" alt="Antonio José de Sucre" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#antonio-jose-de-sucre">Antonio José de Sucre</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antonio_Jos%C3%A9_de_Sucre._Michelena,_Arturo._1895,_Legislative_Palace,_La_Paz.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Arturo Michelena, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bolivia">Bolivia</a> was created from Upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#peru">Peru</a>, the territory of the Royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#audiencia">Audiencia</a> of Charcas, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silver">silver</a> mines at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#potosi">Potosí</a> had made it one of the richest parts of the Spanish empire. The region saw some of the earliest risings for self-government, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sucre">Chuquisaca</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#la-paz">La Paz</a> in 1809, and its rural <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> bands, the <em>republiquetas</em>, fought the royalists for fifteen years, but Spanish armies based in Peru held the main cities until the end of the wars. Upper Peru had been transferred from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru</a> to the new Viceroyalty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a> in 1776, so both Lima and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> claimed it. After the Battle of Ayacucho in December 1824, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#antonio-jose-de-sucre">Antonio José de Sucre</a> advanced into Upper Peru, where the last royalist commander, Pedro Antonio Olañeta, was killed at Tumusla in April 1825. An assembly convened by Sucre in Chuquisaca chose independence rather than union with either neighbour and declared it on 6 August 1825. The new state was named the Republic of Bolívar in honour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#simon-bolivar">the Liberator</a>, soon changed to Bolivia, and Bolívar himself drafted its first constitution in 1826, with a president for life. Sucre became its first constitutional president, and Chuquisaca was later renamed Sucre in his honour.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#potosi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/potosi.jpg" alt="Potosí" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#potosi">Potosí</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vista_de_la_Villa_Imperial_de_Potos%C3%AD.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Parallelepiped09, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bolivia">Bolivia</a> was, and remains, one of the most indigenous countries in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#americas">Americas</a>. At independence most of its roughly one million people were Quechua- or Aymara-speaking peasants living in highland communities, the <em>ayllus</em>, which paid the state a tribute that provided a large share of government revenue until the late nineteenth century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silver">Silver</a> mining at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#potosi">Potosí</a> had collapsed during the wars, and the economy stagnated until a revival of silver production from the 1860s, led by companies such as Huanchaca, linked the country to world markets. Liberal laws from the 1860s to the 1880s, beginning under President Mariano Melgarejo, attacked communal landholding, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hacienda">haciendas</a> spread across the highlands at the expense of the ayllus. From about 1900 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tin">tin</a> replaced silver as the main export, making the mine owners Simón Patiño, Mauricio Hochschild and the Aramayo family the most powerful men in the country and turning mining camps such as Catavi and Siglo XX into centres of militant unionism. The revolution of 1952 nationalised the large mines and distributed hacienda land to peasants. In recent decades natural gas exports to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#brazil">Brazil</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#argentina">Argentina</a>, soya farming in the lowlands around Santa Cruz and coca cultivation have reshaped the economy, and the salt flats of Uyuni hold some of the world’s largest lithium resources.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#andres-de-santa-cruz"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/andres-de-santa-cruz.jpg" alt="Andrés de Santa Cruz" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#andres-de-santa-cruz">Andrés de Santa Cruz</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Andr%C3%A9s_de_Santa_Cruz._Ugalde,_Manuel._c._1835,_Andr%C3%A9s_de_Santa_Cruz_collection,_La_Paz.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Manuel Ugalde, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bolivia">Bolivia</a>’s armies were strongest in its first decades. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#andres-de-santa-cruz">Andrés de Santa Cruz</a> built a disciplined army and in 1836 used it to create the Peru-Bolivian Confederation, but Chile and Peruvian exiles destroyed the confederation at the Battle of Yungay in 1839. In 1841 José Ballivián defeated a Peruvian invasion at the Battle of Ingavi, where the Peruvian president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#agustin-gamarra">Agustín Gamarra</a> was killed. Thereafter the army served mainly as an instrument of coups, and Bolivia lost a series of wars that cost it about half the territory it had claimed at independence. In the War of the Pacific of 1879–1884, which began when Bolivia raised taxes on a Chilean nitrate company in Antofagasta, Chile occupied the entire coast within weeks, and Bolivian troops withdrew from the war after the Battle of Tacna in 1880. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#acre">Acre</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rubber">rubber</a>-rich <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#amazon">Amazon</a> region settled by Brazilians, was lost to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#brazil">Brazil</a> after a rebellion and the Treaty of Petrópolis of 1903. The Chaco War with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#paraguay">Paraguay</a> of 1932–1935, fought over a lowland region believed to hold oil, killed tens of thousands of Bolivian soldiers, many of them highland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#conscription">conscripts</a> unused to the climate, and ended with the loss of most of the Chaco. In 1967 the army defeated Che Guevara’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> band and executed him at La Higuera.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#david"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/david.jpg" alt="David" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#david">David</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_David,_the_King_of_Israel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gerard van Honthorst, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bolivia">Bolivia</a> has had more than a dozen constitutions and was long notorious for frequent coups. Bolívar’s constitution of 1826 was soon abandoned. Santa Cruz, president from 1829 to 1839, organised the treasury, founded universities and introduced civil and penal codes based on the Napoleonic model. For much of the nineteenth century caudillos took power by force, among them the notoriously erratic Melgarejo from 1864 to 1871. After the War of the Pacific a civilian oligarchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silver">silver</a> miners, organised in the Conservative and Liberal parties, ruled through restricted elections. The Federal War of 1898–1899 brought the Liberals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#la-paz">La Paz</a> to power with the help of Aymara forces led by Pablo Zárate Willka, and the seat of government moved to La Paz, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sucre">Sucre</a> remained the constitutional capital. After the Chaco War reforming officers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#david">David</a> Toro and Germán Busch experimented with military socialism. The Revolutionary Nationalist Movement, which came to power in the revolution of April 1952 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#victor-paz-estenssoro">Víctor Paz Estenssoro</a>, introduced universal suffrage, nationalised the mines and carried out land reform in 1953. Military governments dominated from 1964 to 1982, including those of René Barrientos and Hugo Banzer. The 2009 constitution created a plurinational state, recognising Spanish and thirty-six indigenous languages and broad indigenous autonomy.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#evo-morales"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/evo-morales.jpg" alt="Evo Morales" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#evo-morales">Evo Morales</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Evo_Morales_Ayma_(cropped_3).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">EneasMx, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bolivia">Bolivia</a>’s influence has come less from power than from its resources and its political experiments. For three centuries <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#potosi">Potosí</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silver">silver</a> had helped finance the Spanish empire and world trade, and in the twentieth century Bolivian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tin">tin</a> supplied the industries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#united-states">United States</a>, including during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#second-world-war">Second World War</a>. The loss of the Pacific coast in 1879 became a central theme of national identity: Bolivia still keeps a navy on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lake-titicaca">Lake Titicaca</a> and its rivers, marks an annual Day of the Sea, and took its claim to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#international-court-of-justice">International Court of Justice</a>, which ruled in 2018 that Chile was not obliged to negotiate sea access. Bolivia was a pioneer of resource nationalism, confiscating Standard Oil’s concessions in 1937, a year before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mexico">Mexico</a>’s better-known oil expropriation, and the 1952 revolution was one of the most far-reaching social revolutions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#latin-america">Latin America</a> before the Cuban. The election of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#evo-morales">Evo Morales</a> in 2005, the first president to come from the indigenous majority, and the 2009 plurinational constitution made Bolivia a reference point for indigenous political movements across the Andes and beyond. Its Aymara and Quechua cultures, the Carnival of Oruro recognised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO</a>, and the ruins of Tiwanaku give it a distinctive place in the cultural life of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#americas">Americas</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Into the Present</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#sucre"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sucre.jpg" alt="Sucre" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#sucre">Sucre</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sucre,_Capital_de_Bolivia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Parallelepiped09, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The modern Bolivian state was shaped by the 1952 revolution and the turbulent decades that followed. A cycle of military coups, culminating in Luis García Meza’s so-called cocaine coup of 1980, ended with the return of democracy in October 1982 under Hernán Siles Zuazo, whose government was overwhelmed by hyperinflation that exceeded 10,000 per cent in 1985. Paz Estenssoro, returning to power, imposed the New Economic Policy of 1985, closing most state <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tin">tin</a> mines and dismissing more than 20,000 miners, many of whom moved to El Alto or to the coca-growing Chapare. In the 1990s Gonzalo Sánchez de Lozada privatised state companies and decentralised government, but protests against foreign control of resources, notably the Cochabamba Water War of 2000 and the Gas War of 2003, forced him out. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#evo-morales">Evo Morales</a>, leader of the coca growers and of the Movement for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#socialism">Socialism</a> (MAS), governed from 2006 to 2019, nationalising hydrocarbons and using gas revenue to cut poverty sharply. His bid for a fourth term ended in a disputed election and his resignation in 2019. Under Luis Arce falling gas output led to shortages of fuel and dollars, and the 2025 elections ended nearly two decades of MAS government. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bolivia">Bolivia</a> today is a presidential republic of about 11.4 million people, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sucre">Sucre</a> as its constitutional capital and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#la-paz">La Paz</a> as its seat of government.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bolivia">Bolivia</a>, the Andean republic carved from Upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#peru">Peru</a> and named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a>, lost its coast and much of its territory in a series of wars, yet as a major supplier of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silver">silver</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tin">tin</a> and gas and the scene of the 1952 revolution and the first indigenous-led government it has shaped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#latin">Latin</a> American politics.</em></p>

